WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.160
Though you may not have heard of Cameron Boyce&nbsp;
before his tragic passing at the age of 20,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.120
you almost certainly saw his face,&nbsp;
perhaps even earlier than you remember.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.520
While many may recall that Cameron Boyce&nbsp;
was biracial, they may not realize his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.520
grandmother played an important part in American&nbsp;
integration history. The 1954 Supreme Court ruling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:21.520
Brown v. Board of Education established that the&nbsp;
segregation of public schools was unconstitutional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:26.640
under the Fourteenth Amendment, ending the&nbsp;
precedent set by Plessy v. Ferguson in 1896.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.720
As part of public school integration, 12&nbsp;
black children, now known as The Clinton 12,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.480
began attending Clinton High School in Clinton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.680
Tennessee. This monumental change in 1956 created&nbsp;
the state's first integrated public school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.320
and Boyce's paternal grandmother, Jo&nbsp;
Ann Boyce, was one of those students.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:46.080
In 2016, Cameron and his grandmother recounted&nbsp;
the event for Disney Channel's Be Inspired short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.080
series for Black History Month. Speaking&nbsp;
to People about the event, Jo Ann recalled,

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:53.640
"All of us, all of our parents, every single&nbsp;
student that walked down the hill with me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.440
all of our parents wanted us to do better.&nbsp;
They wanted us to have better opportunities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:59.920
so therefore education was number one for them."

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.960
Cameron was evidently proud of the&nbsp;
work his grandmother and her peers did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.840
especially since the public's reaction to&nbsp;
the integration was largely retaliatory.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.720
"They looked like they just wanted to grab&nbsp;
us and throw it. They didn't want us at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.920
I could just see the hate in their heart."

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:14.760
The young actor told People,

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.520
"Things are going to happen in your&nbsp;
life and you're going to face adversity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.640
but if you grow from that and learn from&nbsp;
that, you're a better person because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:24.480
it. [...] How could you not be amazed by&nbsp;
their bravery? She has chosen to forgive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.520
not forget, and she learned from it and&nbsp;
has just grown from her experiences."

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:32.880
Cameron won a Daytime Emmy Award for outstanding&nbsp;
promotional announcement for the segment.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.360
While many know Cameron Boyce for his acting, he&nbsp;
started as a professional dancer. For seven years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.760
he focused almost exclusively on&nbsp;
dance before transitioning into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:45.000
acting. While talking to Young Hollywood&nbsp;
in 2018 about the artform, Boyce noted,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.040
"It actually did come pretty naturally to me.&nbsp;
I, sort of, came out of the womb dancing."

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.200
In a 2019 interview with Glass Magazine,&nbsp;
he even called dance his "first love,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:58.600
a sentiment Boyce's father Victor echoed in a&nbsp;
June 2022 Instagram post memorializing his son.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:03.040
Trained in ballet, breakdancing, hip-hop, jazz,&nbsp;
modern, and tap, Boyce kept dancing at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:07.560
forefront of his career and life. Projects like&nbsp;
the Descendants trilogy continued to let him flex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:12.800
his skills even after he pivoted toward acting.&nbsp;
In a 2018 interview with HERO, he explained,

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.920
"It's really cool to have that in my back&nbsp;
pocket in case I need it. There [are] so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.640
many roles that require dancing or singing&nbsp;
and you have to be able to do all three."

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.280
In addition to using his dance training for&nbsp;
acting roles, he and four childhood friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.760
formed the dance crew X-Mob. Boyce expressed&nbsp;
to HERO that he considered X-Mob some of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:31.800
oldest and closest friends, and the group&nbsp;
continued on even after Boyce's passing.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.520
To celebrate their time with Boyce, X-Mob&nbsp;
posted a longform video to their YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.040
channel featuring clips of the actor dancing&nbsp;
and home videos the group had taken together.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:45.360
A 2008 Panic! at the Disco music video served&nbsp;
as Boyce's first acting job. In the band's video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.560
for the song "That Green Gentleman (Things Have&nbsp;
Changed)," he portrays the child version of Ryan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:54.040
Ross, the band's guitarist at the time. Boyce&nbsp;
is the first character on screen, playing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:58.903
a set of Russian Dolls as the band's lead singer&nbsp;
Brendon Urie's voice comes out of one of the toys.

00:02:58.903 --> 00:03:02.480
In a behind-the-scenes look at the video&nbsp;
uploaded to YouTube by Fueled By Ramen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.600
audiences can see a young&nbsp;
Boyce talking about being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:08.640
in a music video for the first time and&nbsp;
the costume he wears to match Ryan Ross.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.440
"The wardrobe is really&nbsp;
funny, I wouldn't wear this."

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:15.520
This was the first of several music&nbsp;
videos Boyce would appear in. Other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.720
than his own work within the Descendants&nbsp;
franchise, Boyce also turned up in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:22.400
video for "Hardwired" by Hailey Knox as&nbsp;
the male lead, and is credited as one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.960
the lead dancers and choreographers in the&nbsp;
video for Hozier's "Almost (Sweet Music)."

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.800
After the Panic! at the Disco music video,&nbsp;
Boyce's first silver screen appearance was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.160
in a spin-off of the classic medical soap&nbsp;
opera General Hospital. In 2008, He took on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:39.880
the recurring role of Michael "Stone" Cates Jr.&nbsp;
in the short-lived General Hospital: Night Shift.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:43.400
The character was the five-year-old son of&nbsp;
Jagger Cates, one of the main characters in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:47.560
the second season. Over the course of seven&nbsp;
episodes, audiences can watch the young actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:52.560
fine-tune his acting skills as his character goes&nbsp;
on a journey of receiving an autism diagnosis.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.200
While a small role, it started what would become&nbsp;
a fruitful career on TV for Boyce. From there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.920
the young actor guest starred in&nbsp;
the popular Disney Channel series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.560
Good Luck Charlie and Shake It Up before&nbsp;
landing leading TV roles on the network.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.360
The small screen wasn't the only place&nbsp;
audiences could see Boyce. Before winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:11.920
his role in the Adam Sandler-led Grown&nbsp;
Ups, Boyce's first big-screen effort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:17.000
director Alex Aja's 2008 supernatural horror film&nbsp;
Mirrors saw him in the role of Michael Carson.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:21.240
The film's premise involves sinister reflections&nbsp;
killing their real counterparts. Midway through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:25.040
the movie, Michael's reflection begins to&nbsp;
act odd, causing his parents to be concerned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.200
and paint over all the mirrors. However, this&nbsp;
doesn't work for long, as Boyce's character is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:33.280
later found scraping the paint off the mirrors&nbsp;
in a trance-like state. Before the movie ends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.720
Michael's reflection nearly drowns him,&nbsp;
but his mother comes to the rescue.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.520
In 2019, shortly after Cameron's death, Boyce's&nbsp;
father shared an Instagram post that included the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:45.320
Mirrors poster, which depicts the young Boyce&nbsp;
sitting cross-legged on the floor, seemingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:49.960
staring at his reflection in a puddle of water.&nbsp;
Victor Boyce's simple Instagram caption read,

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.760
"Cameron's first movie. He never watched it."

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:56.360
The Disney Channel series Jessie was&nbsp;
Cameron Boyce's big break on the network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.360 --> 00:05:00.120
giving him his first leading role&nbsp;
in a TV series. He played Luke Ross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:04.601
a boy who loves video games and has a particular&nbsp;
fixation with his nanny, the titular Jessie.

00:05:04.601 --> 00:05:08.121
"I'm Luke, and you are?"

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:10.432
"Way too old for you."

00:05:10.432 --> 00:05:13.560
The series was fairly successful for&nbsp;
Disney, making it to a fourth season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:17.200
and airing 98 episodes. However, the show&nbsp;
might not have happened at all without an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:20.600
important conversation between its&nbsp;
lead, Debby Ryan, and a producer.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:24.920
According to an interview with the Star-Telegram,&nbsp;
as Ryan's previous Disney series, The Suite Life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:29.080
on Deck neared its conclusion, Ryan and&nbsp;
executive producer Pamela Eells O'Connell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:33.160
looked at other series for Ryan to star in.&nbsp;
O'Connell had previously worked on The Nanny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:36.560
and the concept for Jessie came up as&nbsp;
the two bounced pitches off each other.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:40.160
Without that conversation, Jessie may not&nbsp;
have come to be, and without the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:44.080
Boyce's first major role may have come&nbsp;
at a very different point in his life.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:48.640
The shift into a leading role in a TV series&nbsp;
impacted many aspects of Boyce's life. In an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:52.040
interview with the Emmys website, the actor&nbsp;
said that he started being homeschooled due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:54.920
to the filming schedule, and that&nbsp;
he even began taking college courses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:59.160
to learn more about responsibility and&nbsp;
accountability in his career. He recalled,

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:02.120
"All the things you are supposed to learn&nbsp;
after you graduate from college I had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:06.560
learn at the ages of 11, 12 and 13. So at this&nbsp;
point in my life I feel that because I was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:09.680
that professional work environment&nbsp;
my whole outlook on life changed."

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:13.080
He acknowledged staying in a more traditional&nbsp;
school setting may have changed who he was as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:17.560
a person, since he wouldn't have had the same&nbsp;
responsibilities he did while starring in Jessie.

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:20.800
Despite everything changing around him,&nbsp;
Cameron Boyce worked to stay grounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:24.160
and not fall victim to the complicated&nbsp;
fates of many Disney actors who came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:27.160
before him. In his interview for&nbsp;
the Emmys website, he explained,

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:30.880
"For the people who view an actor's life as&nbsp;
all glamour and we just want to be famous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:34.680
it's a job that we do, just like any crew&nbsp;
member who has a job. We like to do it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:38.720
not to be in the limelight. It drives me&nbsp;
crazy when actors lose sight of [reality.]"

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.720
Part of not losing sight of reality was&nbsp;
keeping his personal and professional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:44.920
pursuits apart. He explained that,&nbsp;
once shooting wrapped for a day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:48.280
instead of spending the evening reflecting&nbsp;
on his performance, he'd go outside and play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:52.800
basketball. Speaking with Glass Magazine, Boyce&nbsp;
also reflected that, despite his busy schedule,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:56.080
he still tried to find time to feed&nbsp;
his love for dancing whenever he could.

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:59.600
While doing an interview with Schön! Magazine,&nbsp;
Boyce discussed that his parents were the ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:03.360
to instill in him a wariness about his&nbsp;
path as a child star. This caution helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:08.240
Boyce avoid the chaotic and often harmful&nbsp;
situations many other young actors face.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:13.280
Boyce's death came as a shock on July 6,&nbsp;
2019. Just under a month before the release&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:17.120
of the third Descendants movie, the young&nbsp;
actor was found unresponsive in his home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:19.680
which he shared with two of his former castmates.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:22.400
His family made a statement in&nbsp;
the days that followed, saying,

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:24.960
"Cameron's tragic passing was&nbsp;
due to a seizure as a result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:28.480
of an ongoing medical condition,&nbsp;
and that condition was epilepsy."

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:30.120
Until the statement made by his family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:33.000
it was not widely known that the&nbsp;
actor suffered from the disease.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:37.720
An autopsy conducted by the Los Angeles County&nbsp;
Department of Medical Examiner-Coroner initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:41.560
"deferred" the cause and manner of Boyce's&nbsp;
passing. However, after additional testing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:47.200
it was confirmed that the actor suffered a sudden&nbsp;
unexpected death in epilepsy, also known as SUDEP.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:51.560
In the wake of Boyce's untimely passing, the red&nbsp;
carpet premiere for the third Descendants movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:56.160
which was scheduled to come out just weeks later,&nbsp;
was canceled. A few days before the event, Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:59.760
confirmed to The Hollywood Reporter that instead&nbsp;
of hosting the premiere, they were making a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:04.320
donation on Boyce's behalf to the Thirst Project.&nbsp;
The company also stated that, after securing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:09.280
Boyce's family permission, that the broadcast&nbsp;
of Descendants 3 would be dedicated to Boyce.

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:13.920
Boyce's final feature film, Runt, screened in&nbsp;
February 2020 at the Mammoth Film Festival.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:17.200
In the film, Boyce stars as a teen&nbsp;
named Cal who struggles emotionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:21.240
after several difficult events in his life.&nbsp;
Boyce's performance was praised by critics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:23.760
with Jordan Elizabeth writing&nbsp;
for Common Sense Media,

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:26.280
"The star commands the screen&nbsp;
with authenticity and depth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:30.600
slowly transforming as Cal becomes a shell&nbsp;
of his former self with each violent act."

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:36.040
"And what would you even have done, Borgie? Shoot&nbsp;
them with the gun that you stole from your mom?"

00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:40.240
Though Boyce appeared in an episode&nbsp;
of the CBS series Code Black in 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:43.840
his post-Disney career didn't really&nbsp;
start until after his death. His first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:46.800
major series after Jessie and Gamer's&nbsp;
Guide to Pretty Much Everything was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:51.640
the Kathryn Hahn-led HBO miniseries Mrs.&nbsp;
Fletcher, which debuted in October 2019.

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:54.600
Boyce played Zach, the college&nbsp;
roommate of the lead character's son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:58.000
Brendan. When speaking to Haute Living&nbsp;
about his experience filming the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:01.400
Boyce discussed how shooting in New&nbsp;
York City helped inspire him, saying,

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:05.480
"New York is by far the most stimulating city&nbsp;
I've been to. I came back home rejuvenated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:07.200
and more inspired to make art."

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:11.600
Boyce's final performance came out in March&nbsp;
of 2021 with the release of Paradise City on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:15.680
Prime Video. Created and written by Ash&nbsp;
Avildsen, the series is a continuation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:19.800
of the writer's 2017 movie American&nbsp;
Satan. Starring alongside fellow Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:24.520
alum Bella Thorne and Booboo Stewart, Boyce&nbsp;
portrayed Simon, an aspiring young musician.

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:28.360
The series was renewed for a second season,&nbsp;
and Avildsen told Entertainment Weekly that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:32.600
he had many seasons of the show planned before&nbsp;
the first episodes were even filmed. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:36.400
this would require the showrunners to&nbsp;
recast Boyce, though few concrete updates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:40.720
on the development of the next season&nbsp;
have been announced as of Fall 2023.

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:44.440
Cameron Boyce famously worked alongside&nbsp;
Adam Sandler in Grown Ups and Grown Ups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:48.440
2. Boyce played Sandler's on-screen&nbsp;
son, and the two became fast friends.

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:52.040
As part of Adam Sandler's movie deal with&nbsp;
Netflix, the comedic actor made the horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:56.720
comedy Hubie Halloween. The movie follows Hubie,&nbsp;
played by Sandler, as he watches over Halloween&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:01.120
festivities in the town of Salem, Massachusetts.&nbsp;
The film was meant to mark Sandler's reunion with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:05.720
his former co-star, as Boyce was originally&nbsp;
cast as one of Hubie's teen coworkers.

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:08.560
However, Boyce's death resulted&nbsp;
in the part being recast only a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:11.680
few days before the movie was set&nbsp;
to begin filming. In his place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:15.880
Boyce's friend, roommate, and Jessie&nbsp;
co-star Karan Brar was cast in the role.

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:19.800
The film included a tribute to Boyce&nbsp;
during the credits. As an image of Sandler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:24.000
and Boyce appeared on screen, a heartfelt&nbsp;
caption materialized beneath, which read,

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:28.040
"Gone way too soon and one of the kindest,&nbsp;
coolest, funniest, and most talented kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:32.640
we knew. You live on forever in our&nbsp;
hearts and are truly missed every day."

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:37.280
Like many actors, Boyce was a philanthropist and&nbsp;
worked with many organizations. In his time in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:41.160
the spotlight, he raised awareness for causes&nbsp;
such as helping impoverished communities gain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:45.560
access to clean water and trying to end&nbsp;
violence on college campuses nationwide.

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:50.360
Starting in 2014, Boyce was actively involved&nbsp;
with United Way of America's HomeWalk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:54.280
which works to end homelessness. He raised&nbsp;
money for the organization through various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:57.760
campaigns, including offering personal&nbsp;
shout-outs to others raising money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:00.520
and he participated in the opening&nbsp;
ceremonies for the 12th annual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:05.234
HomeWalk in Los Angeles in May 2019&nbsp;
alongside fellow actor Jon Huertas.

00:11:05.234 --> 00:11:09.768
"Every year this makes a big difference,&nbsp;
so I'm really proud to be here.

00:11:09.768 --> 00:11:12.960
The HomeWalk that year raised&nbsp;
over $1.1 million and saw more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:16.480
than 100,000 people participate&nbsp;
in the five-kilometer walk.

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:21.200
Boyce also teamed with It's On Us, an organization&nbsp;
that aspires to end sexual violence on college&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:26.040
campuses. The actor spoke on behalf of the&nbsp;
organization at the 2019 Biden Courage Awards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:30.360
which was hosted by the Biden Foundation&nbsp;
and It's On Us. The ceremony recognized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.920
individuals working toward the organization's&nbsp;
mission through intervention, organizing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:39.920
and policy change. Through his work with It's On&nbsp;
Us, Boyce even met the soon-to-be-President Biden.

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:44.440
At the time of his death, Boyce was working with&nbsp;
Delaney Tarr, co-founder of March For Our Lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:47.720
on a social media campaign contributing&nbsp;
to the effort against gun violence called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:51.360
Wielding Peace. The campaign featured&nbsp;
images of public figures, civilians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:55.760
and victims of gun violence holding household&nbsp;
objects and art supplies as if they were guns.

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:59.480
In a letter shared by a representative, Boyce&nbsp;
explained this concept, and wrote that the goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:03.800
of the campaign was to inspire people to seek&nbsp;
alternative outlets, such as art or fashion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:07.120
through which to express themselves&nbsp;
rather than turning to a deadly weapon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:10.480
The initial photographs, which were later&nbsp;
published by the Cameron Boyce Foundation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:14.000
featured Boyce and his roommates&nbsp;
Karan Brar and Sophie Reynolds.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:18.440
The campaign continued on, with Descendants&nbsp;
co-star Dove Cameron participating in a Wielding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:23.520
Peace campaign to remember Boyce on his 21st&nbsp;
birthday in 2020. In conjunction with the Cameron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:27.520
Boyce Foundation, a merchandise collection&nbsp;
featuring the tattoo Cameron got in honor of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:32.760
the initial campaign helped raise money for both&nbsp;
the foundation and The Actors Fund COVID Relief.

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:36.360
One of the organizations Boyce regularly&nbsp;
worked with was the Thirst Project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:40.520
which works to bring clean water to&nbsp;
underdeveloped countries. In 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:45.600
the young actor used his platform on&nbsp;
Instagram to help raise $27,000 in 40 days.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:49.920
Boyce continued to work with the Thirst Project&nbsp;
until his death, being honored by the organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:55.120
in 2018 with the Pioneering Spirit Award after&nbsp;
raising more than $30,000 to help build two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.120 --> 00:13:00.600
wells in Eswatini. Speaking with Billboard, Thirst&nbsp;
Project's CEO and founder Seth Maxwell explained,

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:03.160
"It was more than an honor for&nbsp;
the money he raised for our work;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:07.000
it was really an honor for a person who knew&nbsp;
no bounds in giving of his time and energy."

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:11.000
After Boyce's death, Adam Sandler hosted a&nbsp;
Facebook fundraiser for the organization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:14.480
which helped raise over $15,000&nbsp;
to build additional wells.

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:19.080
Later in 2019, during the tenth annual Thirst&nbsp;
Gala, the Thirst Project renamed the award&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:23.400
Boyce received as the Cameron Boyce Pioneering&nbsp;
Spirit Award in honor of the actor's memory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:27.080
and his lasting work with the organization.&nbsp;
That year, his roommates presented the award&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:32.600
to Craig Thompson, a YouTuber who raised&nbsp;
more than $150,000 to help build wells.

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:35.920
In an effort to celebrate Cameron&nbsp;
Boyce's memory and philanthropic work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:39.000
the Cameron Boyce Foundation was started in 2019.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:43.360
"The Cameron Boyce Foundation honors&nbsp;
Cameron's legacy by aiming to cure epilepsy."

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:48.240
The Cameron Boyce Foundation helps fund research&nbsp;
and spread information with its Now What campaign.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:52.280
The campaign's goal is to help newly diagnosed&nbsp;
individuals make more informed decisions about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:56.200
their epilepsy and provide a tool kit that can&nbsp;
help patients decide what comes next for them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.200 --> 00:14:00.600
both in their lives and regarding their&nbsp;
health. Based on its 2022 Impact Report,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:04.840
the foundation boasts that more than 800 members&nbsp;
are now part of the Now What support group.

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:08.720
In addition to working to increase awareness&nbsp;
and research for epilepsy, the Cameron Boyce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:12.880
Foundation also celebrates and supports the&nbsp;
other causes Boyce worked for in life. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:17.760
the 10th annual Thirst Gala, the foundation&nbsp;
donated $8,000 to build a well in Uganda.

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:20.880
The organization credited the donations&nbsp;
from their supporters for making the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:24.720
significant donation possible, and pledged&nbsp;
to continue the Thirst Project in Boyce's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:28.200
name. Much like the Wielding Peace&nbsp;
photos, information about the Thirst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:32.160
Project remains on the foundation's&nbsp;
website under the page Cam's Causes.

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:35.000
The Board of Directors includes&nbsp;
Boyce's parents Libby and Victor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:39.520
and Boyce's former castmates Peyton List and&nbsp;
Debby Ryan. Former castmates Dove Cameron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:43.400
Sofia Carson, Booboo Stewart, Adam&nbsp;
Sandler, Karan Brar, Sophie Reynolds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:47.960
and Descendants director Kenny Ortega are&nbsp;
part of the foundation's Advisory Board.

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:51.840
The Cameron Boyce Foundation started&nbsp;
an annual gala, The Cam for A Cause:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:56.560
A Fundraiser for The Cameron Boyce Foundation&nbsp;
to End Epilepsy, in 2022. The gala raises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.560 --> 00:15:00.200
money that the foundation uses to help&nbsp;
fund epilepsy research. Those who knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:03.800
Boyce and other invited parties attend the&nbsp;
event and speak to the foundation's goal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:07.000
share their memories of the late&nbsp;
actor, and support epilepsy research.

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:11.360
In 2023, the gala included the awarding&nbsp;
of the inaugural Youth Empowerment Award,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:15.320
a distinction given to a young individual who&nbsp;
is making a difference in the world. The award&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:19.080
is being used as a way to honor Boyce's&nbsp;
legacy and the work he did in his life.

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:22.680
Yara Shahidi, the first recipient, is&nbsp;
known for her appearance as Tinker Bell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:26.400
in Disney's recent Peter Pan &amp; Wendy movie&nbsp;
and starred in the series Black-ish and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:30.440
Grown-ish. Discussing how Shahidi earned&nbsp;
the award, The Hollywood Reporter wrote,

00:15:30.440 --> 00:15:34.600
"[Shahidi] will be celebrated for her activism&nbsp;
surrounding STEM awareness, women's rights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:38.986
civil rights, and her countless acts of kindness&nbsp;
towards others, according to the foundation."

